  The fabricated [38;5;153muserId[39m is derived from the username so it's stable across calls and obviously fake — consistent with
  the existing stub comment rather than pretending to be a real ID.

  [1mNew file [38;5;153mtracking.js[0m, a plain browser script in the same style as [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — top-level functions, no module system, no
  namespace object:

  [34mfunction[39m [33mtrackLoginEvent[39m({ userId, timestamp }) {
    [32m// Sink seam: replace this with a real telemetry transport.[39m
    console.[33mlog[39m([31m"Login event:"[39m, { userId, timestamp });
  }

  Event fields are [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153mtimestamp[39m only. I left [38;5;153musername[39m out deliberately: [38;5;153muserId[39m already answers "who," and
  carrying both means two identity fields that can disagree.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m gets [38;5;153m<script src="tracking.js"></script>[39m before the [38;5;153mapp.js[39m tag, since plain scripts load in order and
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls into it.

  [1mThe call happens in the form handler, not inside [38;5;153mlogin[39m:[0m

  [34mconst[39m result = [33mlogin[39m(username, password);
  [34mif[39m (result.success) {
    [33mtrackLoginEvent[39m({ [36muserId[39m: result.userId, [36mtimestamp[39m: [34mDate[39m.[33mnow[39m() });
  }
  console.[33mlog[39m([31m"Login result:"[39m, result);

  The reasoning: [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub for a network call. When it becomes real it'll be async, and tracking buried inside it
  would end up tangled in the promise chain. Keeping the call in the handler means authentication does one job and the
  handler decides what to record — so replacing the stub later touches [38;5;153mlogin[39m only.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (login body, handler), [38;5;153mindex.html[39m (one script tag), [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m (new).

